course: dictionary
lesson: 9474
title: Stationery
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Stationery is a noun for things we use to write and do office work: paper, envelopes, pens, pencils, rulers and rubbers. «Pupils need stationery for every lesson.» Stationery is uncountable, so you say some stationery, not a stationery.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «Shops sell it in the stationery aisle.» An aisle where you buy pens and paper tells you what stationery means. Try your meaning in the sentence to check it fits.
explain: Spelling matters. «Stationery» with an e means paper, pens and envelopes: e for envelopes. «Stationary» with an a means standing still. The two words sound the same, so the spelling tells you which one you mean.
fact: Stationery means things used for writing and office work.
fact: Stationery includes paper, envelopes, pens, pencils, rulers and rubbers.
fact: Stationery can also mean writing paper with matching envelopes.
fact: «Stationery» is uncountable, so you say some stationery, not a stationery.
fact: «Stationary» with an a means standing still, so it is a different word.
exercise: «Pupils need stationery for every lesson.» Which of these is stationery? | A football | A bicycle | A bag of crisps | *A ruler
exercise: «Shops sell it in the stationery aisle.» What does the stationery aisle sell? | Shoes and socks | *Pens, paper and envelopes | Garden tools and plants | Bread and milk
exercise: Mia buys 4 pens at 50p each. How much does she pay in total? | £20 | £4 | *£2 | 40p
test: Which word means paper, pens and envelopes? | Stationary | Station | *Stationery | Statuary
test: Which spelling means standing still? | Stationer | *Stationary | Stations | Stationery
exam: Which sentence correctly says the bus was not moving? | The bus bought stationery at the stop. | The bus sold stationery at the stop. | The bus was stationery at the stop. | *The bus was stationary at the stop.
